Z||000005||Lurch||08-05-2001||09:59 PM||||The manual for 90 lumina states that for man trans the ecm needs 24000 ppm.  The chip in the ecm is for an auto trans.  Engine runs fine but seems to idle kinda slow (500-600 rpm) and when decelerating in gear to a stop sign it seems that ecm is cutting fuel to the engine, then it quits unless you give the gas pedal a little blip when you release the clutch before you stop.  Ecm thinks auto trans is in drive all the time, thinking about grounding proper pins to show non-existant auto trans in low gear, but still don't quite get how ecm knows how to terminate decel fuel cutoff without knowing ground speed.  I have 91 beretta manual trans chip, but it requires A/C pressure sensor signal instead of fiero Hi/Lo cutout switches.  I have an A/C pressure sensor and capability to graft it into the A/C, but I wonder if I'll still have the decel fuel cutoff coming into a stop sign?  I had fiero vss teed into 7727 ecm, but speedo was dead until lead to ecm was disconnected (connected backwards?).  Thanks for the replies so far...<P>By the way, 3100 3rd gen. engine makes for very snappy fiero coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||08-05-2001||10:19 PM||||What you need is the IP divisor increased and road speed constant changed in the code. The IPD controls the ECM output to the speedo and the RSC adjusts the ECM's view of the speed.<P>If there isn't any VSS signal or it's too slow, the ECM won't enable DCFO so it's always disabled. The speed must go up and cross a threshold and then DFCO kicks in until the speed crosses the lower threshold. It's nice to have on a manual trans. <P>The normal way to do this is have the VSS drive the ECM and the ECM drive the speedo (the Fiero is backwards). To drive the ECM with ECM output, connect the output through a 10uF cap to the yellow wire of the speedo (or is it purple).... Then, add a 1k resistor from the ECM output to +12V (pullup), then connect a 1k resistor from the yellow or purple speedo input wires to ground. That will give the speedo the AC zero-crossing signal it wants and the ECM gets the pullup it wants.<P>Then the IPD and RSC changes.....<P>What's printed on the top of the mem-cals you are using (on the silver labels)?<P>Are you running DIS and Mass or HEI and Map (SD)?<P>Terry<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-05-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Lurch||08-05-2001||10:29 PM||||I believe the one in the car now is an AUMX; it came with the ecm (90 lumina automatic trans).  The one out of the standard trans beretta is an AUXY.  I had the AUXY in when I first started the car, but took it out when I was trying to charge the AC. It seemed to idle at a more appropriate speed for a manual trans though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Lurch||08-05-2001||10:39 PM||||Terryk, I'm running 3100 with MAP and DIS, just like earlier front drive 3.1.  3100 is out of 96 grand prix; using all the stuff that came on it, except used an earlier plenum with digital EGR.  Used engine harness out of earlier W body car and put ecm where battery used to be on fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||08-06-2001||12:50 AM||||Interesting. I've been hacking on the ARCJ but I'm waiting on a AUMX image. I poke around for the IPD and RSC.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||00lE||08-06-2001||07:28 AM||||      <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lurch:<BR><B>The manual for 90 lumina states that for man trans the ecm needs 24000 ppm.  The chip in the ecm is for an auto trans.  Engine runs fine but seems to idle kinda slow (500-600 rpm) and when decelerating in gear to a stop sign it seems that ecm is cutting fuel to the engine, then it quits unless you give the gas pedal a little blip when you release the clutch before you stop.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you have a scanner, there should be a parameter for "desired rpm".  If you monitor this while at idle and when you drive around, and compare this value to the actual rpm, I think you will find that there is some other problem .  Like Terryk says, without a vss signal there is really no way that the PCM will adjust deceleration fuel parameters. This, in adddition to your low idle rpm values would lead one to the conclusion that the PCM is just not obtaining the its "desired rpm" settings for some other reason.  Without vss, it should behave very similar to a carbed V6, at least that is what I have observed, and I have also not encountered any low idle rpm problems on a setup very similar to yours.<P>I gave this some more thought and looked at my wire harness notes.  I think I have your problem solved.  I ran the power steering pressure sensor [D16] to 5v ref <B>correction: this circuit should run to ground </B> , in anticipation of the "in drive" low rpm idle settings. I think that is why my idle was good although it is a bit high at 960 - 1000.<BR>When the power steering pump presure hits about 500psi (wheels locked all the way in one direction) the pressure sensor closes and the PCM increases the idle rpm to compensate.  By having it always seeing a closed condition, you fool the PCM into using slightly higher idle rpm parameters all the time (since there is no pwr steering pump actually present to lug the engine down).<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||08-06-2001||08:53 PM||||I have a pretty high idle at times,really high if I don't leave the fan on hi,or have the headlights on.<BR>Have been wondering just what could be causing this,since the car runs great otherwise.<BR>So a closed sensor(low pressure) in the PS pressure circuit means low voltage,and a high pressure condition yields +5v in that circuit?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Lurch||08-06-2001||09:06 PM||||Thanks for all the suggestions everybody.<BR>I,ll give some of these a try in the next few days and let you know how it comes out.  This isn't my first engine swap, but it's the first involving computerized engine controls.  It's been interesting and educational, but my wifes patience is growing thin with this project.  Thanks again for the help!||||reg||1
Z||000013||00lE||08-07-2001||04:48 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>So a closed sensor(low pressure) in the PS pressure circuit means low voltage,and a high pressure condition yields +5v in that circuit?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually I was incorrect, it runs to ground, I used the same ground lead as my PCM's sensor ground, and mistook it for the +5v ref on my wiring diagram.  The sensor is normally open, and only connects to ground when the pump sees about 500psi which would indicate that the wheels are locked in one direction or the other and the pump is lugging the engine.  The PCM then increases the idle parameters to prevent the lug from killing the engine.  By running this circuit directly to ground, you (artificially) obtain a slightly higher idle. <P>You may already have done this, as my idle also seems like it could be just a bit lower in order for it to be considered perfect.<P>Sorry about that misinformation, it was way too early in the morning (for me) to be posting a message. :-)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||00lE||08-07-2001||04:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lurch:<BR><B>Thanks for all the suggestions everybody.<BR>I,ll give some of these a try in the next few days and let you know how it comes out.  This isn't my first engine swap, but it's the first involving computerized engine controls.  It's been interesting and educational, but my wifes patience is growing thin with this project.  Thanks again for the help!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Try that power steering pressure circuit thing out, I pulled the pin on it and my idle went extremely low, the engine even killed a few times if I revved it and let it drop back down to idle. I was showing on the scanner as a "desired rpm" of only 600, and my IAC was going crazy trying to keep it there without killing the engine..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||08-07-2001||08:49 AM||||Got my curiosity going anyways,I don't suppose you have any idea what the effects(on running)are from not having the second O2 sensor?<BR>I'm hoping to find something that'll improve the inconsistent idle;it is great/900rpm about 25% of the time,and is like the ecm is searching for something/1200rpm at times(the other 75%).||||reg||1
